bound: this seat sees REWRITES and TRUNCATIONS of records it previously observed, cross-record INCOHERENCE, and — anchored on --boots and the per-boot nonce ledger (--nonces) — APPENDED .bootlog/.recovery growth the host cannot account for. IN THE NONCE REGIME (the host issues fx.bootnonce= per boot and every window boot is covered) the forged line-PAIR in a silent boot's slack is CLOSED: every ADDED line must wear a nonce the host issued for a boot in this window, the per-nonce IN-PROGRESS budget is the boot's OWN line plus one activate RE-ARM per version the store FIRST PUBLISHED in this window (each naming a version newer than the one its boot booted — init.zig:3620), the re-arm ip budget being ONE per first-published VERSION per WINDOW (a publish allocates a version number exactly once) AND naming a version NOT ALREADY BOOTED in the window (a re-arm names the version the activate JUST published, max+1, which has never been booted — this is what refuses the roll-forward REPUBLISH forge), shutdown / .recovery rows hold at one per boot, and the VERDICT accounting is per VERSION — one verdict per in-progress line the window added for that version, plus ONE more ONLY for a first-published version carrying NO re-arm ip of its own (the OLD-writer re-arm shape, init.zig:3693-3696). THE RE-ARM LINE'S OWN DELTA: that legitimate line RE-OPENS the forged-PAIR shape for a version that is first-published in the window AND newer than the boot's own, on ANY real window boot's nonce (the closure there is per VERSION, not per nonce — ONE re-arm in-progress per first-published version per window, so a forged pair cannot ride a second window nonce to buy a second verdict, whether the version was activated or republished — the republish shape is refused by the not-already-booted clause — and the legitimate line grants no verdict slot the +1 did not already grant). RESIDUAL, named: a forged pair for a version first published WITHOUT an activation and NOT YET BOOTED in the window (the operator `rollback` during a live boot, before the next boot boots it) still reads as a legitimate re-arm and lands the version's FIRST verdict — indistinguishable from a real activation without the .operator journal this seat does not read; the automatic roll-forward has no such window (the republish and the boot are one PID1 start), and the moment the version IS booted the not-already-booted clause refuses the same forge. What a forger still cannot mint is an in-progress for a version the store did not publish in this window, or one NOT newer than that nonce's own first line (the per-nonce rule fires on both) — and an unclaimed nonce is REPORTED as a silent boot. What this seat CANNOT see, stated as its bound: a verdict SUBSTITUTED inside a LIVE boot — the agent is root in the guest and can read its own /proc/cmdline nonce, so it can append the verdict itself in the writer's exact shape wearing the live nonce and suppress fx-init's own (a panic before grace expiry); that leaves ONE verdict for one (nonce, version) with every anchor satisfied, and the line's authorship inside the boot that owns the nonce is NOT distinguishable from the host — auditing, not proofing (the defence against substitution is the rollback machinery plus the observation hashes, not this reader); window boots the ledger does not cover (a mixed/manual regime — nonce-less lines are named and counted, but a forged pair in an UNCOVERED boot's slack still passes the arithmetic); boots started off the counting host, or a miscounted / moved --obs-file reuse (the count and the ledger are only as true as the host's own bookkeeping); a coherent tamper on a store first seen at enrollment (the enrollment gap — first run trusts, loudly). That is the honest bound of auditing, not proofing (§6b), printed on every run so the tool never implies more than it knows.
evidence: the store's own records only — attempts.jsonl (the harness self-report) is never opened.
VERDICT: coherent
